            <ArticleTitle>Carotid artery dissection presenting with isolated headache and Horner syndrome after minor head injury.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2103.e5-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ajem.2012.03.010</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0735-6757(12)00114-3</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A woman aged 31 years presented to the emergency department after a minor head injury. She reported mild headache and a metallic taste in her mouth. Full neurologic examination was remarkable only for left-sided Horner syndrome. Left internal carotid artery dissection was confirmed on magnetic resonance imaging. She was treated with aspirin. Symptoms and signs persisted 3 months later, but there was no additional neurologic deficit. We stress the importance of early detection of Horner syndrome to minimize the risk of disabling stroke.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
